1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,125
Welcome to Module Twenty-Six, and to Unit Nine: artificial selection.

2
00:00:05,125 --> 00:00:09,862
Domestic strains are rarely the most impressive members of their lineage.

3
00:00:09,862 --> 00:00:12,687
A bantam is smaller than a junglefowl.

4
00:00:12,687 --> 00:00:16,312
A lab strain of Arabidopsis would lose in the wild.

5
00:00:16,312 --> 00:00:19,237
What you gain is a strain that fits your conditions.

6
00:00:19,237 --> 00:00:22,600
Your bench, your greenhouse, your budget, under your control.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:26,375
Running a language model on your own computer is the same bargain.

8
00:00:26,375 --> 00:00:29,025
You give up the biggest, smartest models.

9
00:00:29,025 --> 00:00:32,437
In return, you get a model that lives on your hardware.

10
00:00:32,437 --> 00:00:34,762
Your data never leaves the machine.

11
00:00:34,762 --> 00:00:36,837
Nobody can change your limits.

12
00:00:36,837 --> 00:00:39,450
An extra request costs nothing.

13
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:43,575
And the model stays exactly the same until you decide to replace it.

14
00:00:43,575 --> 00:00:46,975
One course in this pack is honest about the trade.

15
00:00:46,975 --> 00:00:49,537
You don't go local to save money per word.

16
00:00:49,537 --> 00:00:53,487
You go local for control, and for volume you don't have to meter.

17
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:56,787
Two hardware facts explain almost everything.

18
00:00:56,787 --> 00:01:02,862
To write each word, the program reads every active weight of the model out of memory once.

19
00:01:02,862 --> 00:01:06,500
So the size of your memory decides which models will load at all.

20
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:10,975
And the speed of that memory sets the ceiling on how fast the words come out.

21
00:01:10,975 --> 00:01:14,250
Then there's a trick for fitting a bigger model into less memory.

22
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:16,100
It's called quantisation.

23
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:19,100
Weights normally ship as sixteen-bit numbers.

24
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:24,300
Store them with four bits instead, and a model shrinks to about a third of its size.

25
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:26,300
Often you can't measure the loss.

26
00:01:26,300 --> 00:01:27,787
But not always.

27
00:01:27,787 --> 00:01:37,837
One heavily compressed model in the pack claims ninety-eight percent of the original's average score, and yet on a long, multi-step agent test it falls far behind.

28
00:01:37,837 --> 00:01:40,925
Averages can hide the one drop you care about.

29
00:01:40,925 --> 00:01:43,087
So you test on your own tasks.

30
00:01:43,087 --> 00:01:46,662
There's one more memory cost people forget: the context.

31
00:01:46,662 --> 00:01:50,512
Every word the model is holding in its working memory takes space.

32
00:01:50,512 --> 00:01:55,237
Raise the context window, and memory climbs, even if your prompt is short.

33
00:01:55,237 --> 00:02:01,875
In the lab, you'll install a free local runtime and a small model of about four billion parameters.

34
00:02:01,875 --> 00:02:06,212
Then you'll run a careful experiment, the way you would in a growth chamber.

35
00:02:06,212 --> 00:02:08,350
Change one setting at a time.

36
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:10,687
Short prompt versus long prompt.

37
00:02:10,687 --> 00:02:12,975
Small context versus large.

38
00:02:12,975 --> 00:02:14,850
Three replicates each.

39
00:02:14,850 --> 00:02:18,075
Record reading speed, writing speed and memory.

40
00:02:18,075 --> 00:02:27,162
And finally, run the ten held-out items from your Module Twenty-Three eval through the local model, and compare its accuracy with what you measured before.

41
00:02:27,162 --> 00:02:34,150
By the end, you'll know whether this little strain is good enough for the simple, repetitive decisions in your future capstone.

42
00:02:34,150 --> 00:02:40,287
Start with Part A. Find out how much memory your computer has, and work out the biggest model it should hold.

